ushed tens of thousands of people  into poverty and triggered the largest  anti-government protests in more than a  decade high inflation and unemployment  shortages of food and medicine are just  some of the problems affecting  millions and as redmond shannon reports  there's no solution  in sight lebanon has seen plenty of  hardship  over the years from its own civil war to  the more  recent influx of refugees from syria  now the country is on its knees again  at least one man appeared to have had  enough protesters  blaming his suicide on desperation  poverty  and hunger the economic and political  crisis  ramped up last fall with protests  triggered by proposed  new taxes and then came kobit 19  suddenly tipping the economy over the  edge so the people that used to be  let's say middle class now they don't  exist anymore  middle class has disappeared because  everyone needs help  lebanon imports almost everything but  the value of its currency has  effectively dropped to one  sixth of its official value banks are  limiting withdrawals  stores have nothing to sell hospitals  are  rationing treatments over 40  50 percent of people unable to access  food water drinkable water  we are in debt just to buy bread eggs  and cheese  says this woman with money almost  worthless  some people are bartering for food on  facebook groups  protesters blame incompetence and  corruption  demanding a total overhaul to lebanon's  unique form of government  a system that means it is always being  torn between regional and  global powers namely iran saudi arabia  and the west there is no easy solution  to this  that does not require turning a blind  eye to bolstering elements of lebanese  society that have been part  of the problem there is no international  will at all from any side  in order to allow the people to manifest  their will to overhaul that system  a bailout from the international  monetary fund has been stalled  too with disagreements over the scale of  the funds needed